WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.660
Operators need enough visibility to distinguish service problems, model problems,

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.520
client problems, and hardware problems.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:10.640
When a local AI workflow fails, the visible symptom may be vague: a slow response,

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.000
a missing model, an application timeout, or a user report that the tool feels different.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.600
Troubleshooting is much easier when the environment has a simple operating picture.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.160
Useful troubleshooting starts with service status, logs, model inventory,

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:24.555
storage availability, and resource utilization.

00:00:24.555 --> 00:00:27.120
Is the service running? Is the expected model present?

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.070
Is there enough disk space? Is memory under pressure?

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:32.260
Is CPU or GPU utilization unusually high?

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.155
Did a configuration value change?

00:00:34.155 --> 00:00:38.635
These basic checks can explain many issues before teams start changing application code or

00:00:38.635 --> 00:00:39.470
model settings.

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:43.420
Slow responses may come from model loading, insufficient memory, long prompts,

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:48.435
high concurrency, CPU or GPU pressure, thermal throttling, or client-side expectations.

00:00:48.435 --> 00:00:52.600
Failed pulls or missing models may point to connectivity, permissions, naming, storage,

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.480
or environment configuration issues.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.455
A troubleshooting checklist can reduce repeated support work and help new operators build

00:00:58.455 --> 00:00:59.130
confidence.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:02.070
Logging should support investigation without creating a data leak.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:05.980
Logs may reveal service errors, model loading behavior, configuration problems,

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:07.320
or integration failures.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:11.570
They should not casually store secrets, credentials, personal data, confidential prompts,

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:12.710
or sensitive outputs.

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:16.300
The best troubleshooting practice is to gather enough evidence to act while still

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.720
respecting privacy and data boundaries.
